Operating Systems: Short Design Note

Topics: CPU Scheduling, Page Replacement, Semaphores, Thrashing, and RAID

Purpose

This note works through one CPU scheduling example and one page-replacement example, then gives implementation guidance for a bounded buffer, a practical thrashing diagnosis plan, and a RAID comparison for a small lab cluster.

---

1. CPU Scheduling: Round Robin

Problem

Use Round Robin scheduling with a time quantum of 2 time units. All times are in the same units.

Process| Arrival Time| CPU Burst
P1| 0| 5
P2| 1| 3
P3| 2| 1

Scheduling convention: At a quantum boundary, processes that have arrived are placed in the ready queue in arrival order; an unfinished process goes to the tail. The CPU is idle only if the ready queue is empty.

Hand Simulation

- 0–2: P1 runs; 3 units remain. P2 arrives at 1 and P3 at 2.
- 2–4: P2 runs; 1 unit remains.
- 4–5: P3 runs and finishes.
- 5–7: P1 runs; 1 unit remains.
- 7–8: P2 runs and finishes.
- 8–9: P1 runs and finishes.

Gantt Chart

0       2       4   5       7   8   9
|  P1   |  P2   | P3|  P1   | P2| P1|

The chart shows the order and duration of each execution interval.

- Completion Time (CT): Time at which a process finishes.
- Turnaround Time (TAT): CT − Arrival Time.
- Waiting Time (WT): TAT − CPU Burst.

Results

Process| Arrival| Burst| CT| TAT = CT − AT| WT = TAT − BT
P1| 0| 5| 9| 9 − 0 = 9| 9 − 5 = 4
P2| 1| 3| 8| 8 − 1 = 7| 7 − 3 = 4
P3| 2| 1| 5| 5 − 2 = 3| 3 − 1 = 2

Average turnaround time

(9 + 7 + 3) / 3 = 19 / 3 ≈ 6.33 time units

Average waiting time

(4 + 4 + 2) / 3 = 10 / 3 ≈ 3.33 time units

Interpretation

Round Robin is designed to provide responsive, fair CPU sharing, especially for interactive workloads. It is not guaranteed to minimize average waiting time. A very small time quantum improves responsiveness but increases context-switch overhead, while a very large quantum behaves more like First-Come, First-Served (FCFS).

