3. Correct Bounded Buffer Using Semaphores

Problem

Design a bounded buffer of fixed capacity "N" that supports one or more producers and one or more consumers. Producers insert items, while consumers remove them.

The solution must prevent buffer overflow, buffer underflow, and simultaneous unsafe access to shared buffer data.

Semaphore Initialization

Use three semaphores:

- "empty = N": Counts available buffer slots.
- "full = 0": Counts occupied buffer slots.
- "mutex = 1": Protects the shared buffer and its indices.

Producer Pseudocode

Producer:
    while true:
        item = produce_item()

        wait(empty)       # Reserve an empty slot
        wait(mutex)       # Enter critical section

        insert(item)      # Add item to buffer

        signal(mutex)     # Leave critical section
        signal(full)      # Publish the available item

Consumer Pseudocode

Consumer:
    while true:
        wait(full)        # Reserve an available item
        wait(mutex)       # Enter critical section

        item = remove()   # Remove item from buffer

        signal(mutex)     # Leave critical section
        signal(empty)     # Publish the free slot

        consume_item(item)

Required Wait Order

The correct order is:

- Producer: "wait(empty)" before "wait(mutex)".
- Consumer: "wait(full)" before "wait(mutex)".

Release the mutex before signaling the opposite counting semaphore. Never wait on "empty" or "full" while holding the mutex.

Why This Is Correct

1. The counting semaphores prevent inserting into a full buffer and removing from an empty buffer.
2. The mutex ensures only one thread updates the shared buffer and indices at a time.
3. Reserving a slot or item before acquiring the mutex avoids a classic deadlock in which a producer holds the mutex while blocked because the buffer is full, preventing a consumer from removing an item.
4. Every successful insertion is eventually paired with a removal, assuming the system continues to make progress and operations complete.

Invariant

For a buffer of capacity "N", the semaphore counts correspond to available and occupied slots. In normal operation:

"empty + full = N"

This equality can temporarily differ while threads are between semaphore operations because a slot or item may already have been reserved.

Implementation Cautions

- Semaphore operations must be atomic and provide the required memory-ordering guarantees.
- If insertion or removal can fail, handle that path without leaving a slot reservation or mutex locked.
- Do not signal "full" before the item is fully inserted.
- Do not signal "empty" before removal is complete.
- For cancellation or shutdown, define an explicit protocol, such as a stop item or a separate shutdown condition, so blocked threads can exit safely.

