5. RAID Comparison for a Small Lab Cluster

Scenario

Assume a small lab cluster needs dependable storage for student virtual machines, project repositories, and datasets.

Compare RAID 1 (mirroring) with RAID 5 (striping with distributed parity).

Important: RAID is not a backup. It does not protect against accidental deletion, ransomware, controller mistakes, or site loss.

Comparison Table

Factor| RAID 1| RAID 5
Minimum drives| 2| 3
Usable capacity| About 50% of raw capacity for a two-drive mirror| Approximately the capacity of N − 1 drives
Drive-failure tolerance| One drive per mirror pair; depends on which drives fail| One drive in the array
Performance| Good reads; writes go to both mirror copies| Good reads; small writes incur parity work
Rebuild and risk| Usually simpler and often quicker for a small mirror| Rebuild can take longer; a second drive failure during rebuild can cause data loss
Best fit| Small, latency-sensitive storage where simplicity matters| Capacity-efficient bulk storage when three or more drives are available

Recommendation

For a very small lab cluster with only two drives and a preference for simplicity, RAID 1 is the practical choice.

If the cluster has at least three suitable drives and usable capacity per cost is more important, RAID 5 can be considered, provided the workload is not dominated by small synchronous writes and rebuild risk is acceptable.

For write-heavy virtual-machine storage, evaluate RAID 10 or a modern fault-tolerant storage design as well.

RAID Deployment Checklist

Before choosing a RAID level, verify the actual drive count, drive sizes, workload, controller or software RAID support, and expected rebuild time.

- RAID 1 capacity is limited by the smaller member of the mirror.
- RAID 5 capacity is approximately the combined capacity of all drives except one drive's worth of parity.
- Mixing drive sizes can reduce usable capacity.
- Use suitable drives and a controller/software stack with health monitoring.
- Monitor device health, array degradation, rebuild progress, and storage latency.
- Keep tested, separate backups and periodically test restoration.
- Document the failure procedure and replacement-drive process.
- Consider whether storage must remain available through a host failure; local RAID alone does not provide shared-storage high availability.

